Programming in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>Appendix D - Working with Edit and HostFS</H1>
<HR>
This appendix collects tips for writing programs in Edit, for running them from a task window, and for
keeping your programs on the computer your web browser is running on, where you can use another editor if
you prefer.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Edit for programmers</H2>
<H3>Making and opening programs</H3>
<UL>
<LI>To start a new program, click Menu on the Edit icon, move to <I>Create</I>, type <CODE>JSScript</CODE> in
the box at the bottom of the menu and press Return. Edit remembers what you typed, so next time the box
already says JSScript.<P>
<LI>Double-clicking a program runs it. To open it in Edit instead, hold down Shift while you double-click
it, or drag it onto the Edit icon on the icon bar.<P>
<LI>If you saved a program as a Text file by mistake, change its type with <I>Menu &gt; Misc &gt; Set
type</I> in Edit (type <CODE>JSScript</CODE> and press Return, then save it again), or with <I>Set type</I>
on the Filer's <I>File</I> menu.<P>
</UL>
<H3>Useful keys</H3>
<PRE>
F3           save (Return in the Save box saves it again
             under the same name)
F4           find, and find and replace
F5           go to a line (the box shows the caret's line)
F8  F9       undo, redo
Ctrl-F2      close the window
Copy         delete the character to the right of the caret
Ctrl-Left    start of the line   Ctrl-Right   end of the line
Ctrl-Up      start of the file   Ctrl-Down    end of the file
Shift-Up     page up             Shift-Down   page down
Shift-F3     turn Column tab on or off
</PRE>
<H3>Indenting</H3>
The Tab key in Edit doesn't put a tab character in your program. Instead it moves the caret along to line
up with the start of the next word on the line above. So when the line above is indented, pressing Tab at
the start of a new line takes you to the same indent - which is what you want when indenting a program.
Press Tab, then type (and add or remove two spaces when the indent should change).<P>
If you'd rather Tab moved to the next multiple of eight columns, turn on <I>Column tab</I> (Shift-F3, or
<I>Menu &gt; Misc &gt; Column tab</I>). Either way Edit puts spaces in the file, which is what this book's
programs use.<P>
<H3>Keeping lines short</H3>
Edit wraps a line only when it reaches the width of the screen, so a long line runs off the right of a
narrower window. Keep lines to 70 characters or so, splitting a long statement after an operator or a comma
(JavaScript doesn't mind), and make the window wide enough to see them.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Running programs from a task window</H2>
A task window is a window where you can type <CODE>*</CODE> commands while the desktop carries on. Press
Ctrl-F12 to open one, then run a program by typing its name after <CODE>JSRun</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
*JSRun HardDisc4.$.MyProgs.Hello
</PRE>
Typing the full name each time is tedious, so first make your directory the <I>current directory</I> with
<CODE>*Dir</CODE>; after that the program's own name is enough:<P>
<PRE>
*Dir HardDisc4.$.MyProgs
*JSRun Hello
*JSRun Greet
</PRE>
A program run from a task window prints in the task window, and <CODE>input</CODE> reads what you type
there. Anything after the program's name is passed to it in <CODE>ctx.args</CODE> (and, split into words,
<CODE>ctx.argv</CODE>), so a program run with<P>
<PRE>
*JSRun MyProgram red 12
</PRE>
finds <CODE>'red 12'</CODE> in <CODE>ctx.args</CODE> and <CODE>['red', '12']</CODE> in
<CODE>ctx.argv</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Keeping programs on your own computer</H2>
Your programs are normally kept on the desktop's hard disc, which your web browser stores for you. With
<B>HostFS</B> you can keep them in a folder on your own computer instead. They are then safe even if the
browser's stored data is cleared, and you can edit them with any editor you like, then run them in RISC OS.
<CODE>HardDisc4.$.Docs.HostFS</CODE> explains HostFS fully; here is how to use it for programs.<P>
<H3>Mounting a folder</H3>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Make a folder on your computer for your programs.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Click Select on the HostFS icon on the icon bar and choose the folder. (This needs Chrome, Edge or
another Chromium browser. In other browsers, start the desktop's server with the folder named, as the
HostFS guide explains.)<P>
<DT><DD>3  The folder appears on the icon bar with its name, for example <CODE>Progs</CODE>, and as a disc,
<CODE>HostFS::Progs.$</CODE>. Click it to open it.<P>
</DL>
<H3>Program names</H3>
Your computer keeps file types in file names, so a JavaScript program there needs a name ending
<CODE>.js</CODE>, such as <CODE>hello.js</CODE>. RISC OS shows it as <CODE>hello/js</CODE>, with the
JSScript icon, and double-clicking it runs it. Programs you save into the folder from Edit are named for you:
save a JSScript file called <CODE>hello/js</CODE> and it appears on your computer as
<CODE>hello.js</CODE>; save one called just <CODE>Hello</CODE> and it appears as <CODE>Hello,f81</CODE>
(<CODE>,f81</CODE> is how HostFS records the file type when the name doesn't show it).<P>
In a module, import another file as usual. <CODE>'./Board'</CODE> finds a file called <CODE>Board</CODE> or
<CODE>Board/js</CODE>, so the same program works whether its files have the ending or not; you can also write
the ending, as programmers elsewhere do:<P>
<PRE>
import { drawBoard } from './Board.js';     // Board/js (or Board)
</PRE>
<H3>Applications</H3>
Application directories work in a HostFS folder too, but their special files need their types recorded in
their names: <CODE>!Run,feb</CODE> (Obey), <CODE>!RunImage,f81</CODE> (JSScript), <CODE>!Sprites,ff9</CODE>
(Sprite). The easiest way to get them right is to make the application in RISC OS, or copy one such as
!Snake into the HostFS folder with the Filer: HostFS names every file for you.<P>
<H3>Editing on your computer</H3>
Open the <CODE>.js</CODE> file in your computer's editor, change it and save it. RISC OS sees the change
when you next open the directory display, or when you come back to the browser window, and double-clicking
the program always runs the latest version. Most programmers' editors also colour JavaScript's keywords and
strings, and point out many syntax mistakes as you type, which Edit can't do.<P>
<B>Note</B>: if you edit the same program in Edit and on your computer at the same time, whichever saves
last wins. Close it in one before changing it in the other.<P>

